# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[number#Noun|number]] [[nine]].
#: {{synonyms|en|nonad}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Proclus|authorlink=Proclus|author2=[[w:Thomas Taylor (neoplatonist)|Thomas Taylor]], transl.|chapter=XXXI|title=The Six Books of Proclus the Platonic Successor, on the Theology of Plato, Translated from the Greek; {{...|to which a Seventh Book is Added, in Order to Supply the Deficiency of Another Book on This Subject, which was Written by Proclus, but since Lost. Also, a Translation from the Greek of Proclus’ Elements of Theology. To which are Added a Translation of the Treatise of Proclus, on Providence and Fate; a Translation of Extracts from His Treatise, Entitled, Ten Doubts Concerning Providence; and a Translation of Extracts from His Treatise on the Subsistence of Evil; as Preserved in the Bibliotheca Gr. of Fabricus. [...]}} Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed for the author, by [[w:Abraham John Valpy|A[braham] J[ohn] Valpy]],{{nb...|Tooke's Court, Chancery Lane. And sold by Messrs. Law and Co.; Longman and Co.; Baldwin and Co.; and all other booksellers.}}|year=1816|volume=II|section=footnote 1|page=211|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=s6AtOsizyhgC&pg=PA211|oclc=28259674|passage=The '''ennead''', according to the [[Pythagorean]]s, circulates all numbers within itself, and there can be no number beyond it. For the natural progression of numbers is as far as to 9, but after it their retrogression takes place. For 10 becomes as it were again the monad. {{...}} Hence it is not possible there should be any elementary number beyond the '''ennead'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Hippolytus|authorlink=Hippolytus of Rome|author2=[[w:John Henry MacMahon|J[ohn] H[enry] MacMahon]]|chapter=XLIII|editors={{w|Alexander Roberts}} and [[w:James Donaldson (classical scholar)|James Donaldson]]|title=[[w:Refutation of All Heresies|The Refutation of All Heresies,{{nb...|[...] With Fragments from His Commentaries on Various Books of Scripture, Translated by the Rev. S[tewart] D[ingwall] F[ordyce] Salmond.}}]]|series=[[w:Ante-Nicene Fathers|Ante-Nicene Christian Library]]:{{nb...|Translations of the Writings of the Fathers down to A.D. 325}}|seriesvolume=VI (Hippolytus, Bishop of Rome. Vol. I.)|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:T&T Clark|T. & T. Clark]],{{nb...|38, George Street}}; London: Hamilton & Co.; Dublin: John Robertson & Co.|year=1868|section=book IV (Of the Refutation of All Heresies)|page=111|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pX1PAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA111|oclc=490122286|passage=[T]he '''ennead''' is subtracted for this cause, because the three hundred and sixty parts of the entire [circle] consist of '''enneads''', and for this reason the four regions of the world are circumscribed by ninety perfect parts.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} Any [[grouping#Noun|grouping]] or [[system]] containing nine [[object#Noun|objects]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Edward Greswell|authorlink=Edward Greswell|chapter=On the Lunar in Contradistinction to the Solar Modifications of the Primitive Calendar|title=Fasti Temporis Catholici and Origines Kalendariæ. [...] In Four Volumes|location=Oxford|publisher=At the [[w:Oxford Universty Press|University Press]]|year=1852|volume=I|section=section V (The Octaëteris)|pages=566–567|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=L9xSAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA567|oclc=457298519|passage=We may assert with confidence that no cycle of this kind, and of equal antiquity or even of inferior antiquity, is the actual existence better attested or more thoroughly authenticated than that of this [[octaeteris|octaëteris]] of Philammon of Delphi, or, as we may truly call it, this original Pythian '''ennead''' of primitive Hellas.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal={{w|The Church Quarterly Review}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge}}|year=1882|volume=XIII|page=172|oclc=567556919|passage=The exquisite language of the prophecy of {{w|Isaiah}}, especially in its last three '''enneads''', may have had a nearer primary reference to, and a nearer fulfilment in, the return of the exiles under {{w|Zerubbabel}}, and {{w|Ezra}}, and {{w|Nehemiah}}; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=E[rnest] A[lfred] Wallis Budge|authorlink=E. A. Wallis Budge|chapter=The Babylonian Religion|title=Babylonian Life and History|series=By-paths of Bible Knowledge|seriesvolume=V|location=London|publisher={{w|Religious Tract Society}},{{nb...|56, Paternoster Row at 65, St. Paul’s Churchyard.}}|year=1884|pages=127–128|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/babylonianlifea03budggoog/page/n150/mode/1up|oclc=3165864|passage=At one time one god was a greater favourite with the nation than another, and the cult of the god or gods which the old Babylonians preferred in the early days of their empire frequently fell into disuse and neglect in after times. The most important '''ennead''' among the Babylonians was as follows:— {{...}}}}

